A||N||5||bilbo||Overheating problem.........help!!!||||1||
Z||000000||bilbo||05-30-2001||01:23 PM||||I think I may have a clogged radiator.Engine cooling tubes are hot but radiator is cool.Can someone give the proper steps to do a 2.8 cooling system flush.I did a search and I could only find something for a 4 cylinder.Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Carrolles||05-30-2001||01:37 PM||||If the cooling tubes are hot then coolant must be running through the radiator.  Maybe there is a large air bubble in the radiator.  Try burping the air out of the system.<P>Park the car with the nose slightly down.  With the system cool open the radiator cap up front and remove the cap from the thermostat housing and pull out the thermostat.  Pour coolant into the T-stat housing until it starts to come out of the radiator cap.  Stop and cap the radiator.  Now continue to fill from the T-stat housing until full.  Start the engine and let coolant circulate for a bit to work any remaining air from the system.  Top off at the T-stat housing as needed.<P>Look to see that coolant is actually moving inside the T-stat housing to see if the water pump is working ok.<P>Re-install the thermostat and its cap (it helps to put a little vaseline on the t-stat o-ring).<P>Make sure the radiator cap is "NON-VENTED".  The Stant cap that AutoZone and other chain parts stores lists for Fieros is a "VENTED" cap which is incorrect.  A vented cap will let coolant syphon through the overflow reservoir if the car is parked with the nose slightly down.<P>If your problem still exists after this you might pull the radiator and take it to a shop to be rodded out.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||bilbo||05-30-2001||01:53 PM||||I will try this tomorrow,thanks for the reply. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||05-30-2001||01:58 PM||||Make sure the system is properly topped off and the overflow is full. If you have AC run the engine until hot then turn the engine off and switch the key back on with the A/C on. this will run the fan and cool the radiator give it a minute and squeeze the radiator hose, if its mushey or collapses then you have air in the system and need to top off the coolant. The coolant hose should stay firm at all times until the engine is cooled down quite a bit.<P>My method of filling the coolant is to remove the front and rear caps, Fill the rear until it overflows out the front. put the front cap back on the fill the rear and put it's cap back on and fill the overflow. Run the engine until hot let it cool for a few minutes and then crack the front cap to let the air out(HOT SO BE CAREFULL!!!) go to the rear and refill the coolant. run the engine again stop it then undo the upper heater hose ( be carefull not to twist heater line to much since this can damage the heater core) until coolant flows out. refill and run again until hot and shut it down and let it fully cool.<P>Just remember to let it cool off a bit before you go opening up the system. The idea is to have a little pressure in the system but not to have boiling hot coolant gushing out when you open it. A good way to test for pressure is to squeeze the hose. if it's really hard then just let it cool a bit more. Patience is key when burping the system.<P>(NOTE) the gaskets on both caps should be pliable if they are hard just replace them, Hard gaskets make it really! hard to remove and install the caps.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/sig.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Fiero_24||05-30-2001||04:06 PM||||Why not wire in a switch to make the front fan go on earlier?  I add this switch on every Fiero I buy.  Look at the harness by the gass pedal in the front wall.  From the bottom right go up (I think 3) wires and it should be green.  Attach a wire and then to a switch, then to a ground.  This way you can run the fan when you want.  Or if you are out and the temp creeps up turn on the max air (if it works or not)  and it will turn on the fan.  I had an 86 SC that would overheat all the time until I added the switch.  E-mail me if I was unclear at all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||05-30-2001||06:32 PM||||check the radiator for consistencty in temp across it.  the bottom my be clogged and coolant is only flowing of the top.  if ou put your hand on it and its cold at the bottom but you cant even touch the top thats most likely what you have.  in that case try flushing it.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <P>88FORMULA<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>       <P>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">           <BR>"Kids in the backseat cause accidents,  accidents in the backseat caused me!!"||||reg||1
